{"id":"feature-scaling","t":"Standard, min-max and robust scaling","tags":["Feature Engineering","Data Processing"],"d":"Easy","rt":"numpy","fn":"scale","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"Write `scale(X_train, X_test, method)` that learns a per-column shift $c_j$ and scale $s_j$ from `X_train`, then returns $(X - c)/s$ for both arrays.\n\n| `method` | shift $c_j$ | scale $s_j$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `'standard'` | mean | standard deviation with `ddof=0` |\n| `'minmax'` | minimum | maximum minus minimum |\n| `'robust'` | median | interquartile range $Q_{75} - Q_{25}$ |\n\n## Rules\n- Quantiles use NumPy's default linear interpolation (`np.percentile(col, [25, 75])`).\n- If a column's scale is 0 (for example a constant column), use $s_j = 1$ for it, so that column is just shifted.\n- Statistics come from `X_train` only; apply the same $c$ and $s$ to `X_test`. Test values may land outside $[0, 1]$ for `minmax`; that's expected.\n\n## Input / output\n`X_train`: float `(n, d)` with $n \\ge 1$; `X_test`: float `(m, d)`. Return `(train_scaled, test_scaled)` as float arrays of the input shapes. Vectorise over columns.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef scale(X_train, X_test, method):\n    \"\"\"Fit per-column shift/scale on X_train ('standard', 'minmax' or 'robust'); return (train_scaled, test_scaled).\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["Each method is just a choice of two length-$d$ vectors. Compute them with `axis=0` reductions on the training data.","`np.std` defaults to `ddof=0`, which is what's asked. `np.percentile(X, [25, 75], axis=0)` returns both quartile rows at once.","Guard the division with `np.where(s == 0, 1.0, s)`, then apply the same `(X - c) / s` to train and test."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef scale(X_train, X_test, method):\n    X, Xt = np.asarray(X_train, dtype=float), np.asarray(X_test, dtype=float)\n    if method == 'standard':\n        c, s = X.mean(axis=0), X.std(axis=0)\n    elif method == 'minmax':\n        c, s = X.min(axis=0), X.max(axis=0) - X.min(axis=0)\n    else:\n        q25, q75 = np.percentile(X, [25, 75], axis=0)\n        c, s = np.median(X, axis=0), q75 - q25\n    s = np.where(s == 0, 1.0, s)                           # constant columns: shift only\n    return (X - c) / s, (Xt - c) / s\n","ex_md":"## Why scale at all\nDistance-based models (k-NN, k-means, SVMs with RBF kernels) and penalised models (ridge, lasso) treat all features on the same footing, so a feature measured in thousands would dominate one measured in fractions. Gradient descent also converges faster on well-conditioned, similarly scaled inputs. Tree models are invariant to monotone per-feature transforms and don't need it.\n\n## Which scaler\n- **Standard** gives zero mean, unit variance. It's sensitive to outliers, which inflate the standard deviation.\n- **Min-max** maps training data into $[0, 1]$. One extreme value squashes everything else into a tiny range.\n- **Robust** uses the median and IQR, so the outlier at 100 in test 3 barely moves the other values' scaled positions.\n\n## The zero-scale guard\nA constant training column (test 4, and column 3 of the random tests) has zero spread. Dividing by it gives `inf`/`nan`. Using a scale of 1 keeps the column finite; dropping it is also common.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Fitting the scaler on the full dataset or on the test set (leakage).\n- `ddof=1` instead of `ddof=0`.\n- Scaling per row (`axis=1`) instead of per column.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Should you scale one-hot encoded columns? What about the target in regression?\n- With cross-validation, where exactly must the scaler be fitted? (Inside each fold, on that fold's training part only, which is what a pipeline object guarantees.)","tests":[{"a":[{"__nd__":[[1.0,10.0],[3.0,20.0],[5.0,30.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[2.0,40.0]],"dtype":"float64"},"standard"],"e":{"__tup__":[{"__nd__":[[-1.224744871391589,-1.224744871391589],[0.0,0.0],[1.224744871391589,1.224744871391589]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[-0.6123724356957945,2.449489742783178]],"dtype":"float64"}]}},{"a":[{"__nd__":[[1.0,10.0],[3.0,20.0],[5.0,30.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[2.0,40.0]],"dtype":"float64"},"minmax"],"e":{"__tup__":[{"__nd__":[[0.0,0.0],[0.5,0.5],[1.0,1.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[0.25,1.5]],"dtype":"float64"}]}},{"a":[{"__nd__":[[1.0],[2.0],[3.0],[4.0],[100.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[0.0],[50.0]],"dtype":"float64"},"robust"],"e":{"__tup__":[{"__nd__":[[-1.0],[-0.5],[0.0],[0.5],[48.5]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[-1.5],[23.5]],"dtype":"float64"}]}},{"a":[{"__nd__":[[4.0,1.0],[4.0,2.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[5.0,0.0]],"dtype":"float64"},"standard"],"e":{"__tup__":[{"__nd__":[[0.0,-1.0],[0.0,1.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[1.0,-3.0]],"dtype":"float64"}]}},{"a":[{"__nd__":[[9.0,-3.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[10.0,-3.0]],"dtype":"float64"},"minmax"],"e":{"__tup__":[{"__nd__":[[0.0,0.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[1.0,0.0]],"dtype":"float64"}]}},{"a":[{"__nd__":[[1000000000.0,-1e-09],[3000000000.0,1e-09],[2000000000.0,0.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[0.0,2e-09]],"dtype":"float64"},"standard"],"e":{"__tup__":[{"__nd__":[[-1.2247448713915892,-1.2247448713915892],[1.2247448713915892,1.2247448713915892],[0.0,0.0]],"dtype":"float64"},{"__nd__":[[-2.4494897427831783,2.4494897427831783]],"dtype":"float64"}]}},{"a":[{"__nd__":[[66.72813454007384,0.16304054396463916,7.0,0.0],[49.10839975532338,1.1637245769199653,7.0,0.0],[42.698641474303145,0.3859805000912195,7.0,2.0],[60.08304843892595,0.49657180421238645,7.0,0.0],[45.29462377732801,1.2837581901327157,7.0,2.0],[39.99388509083521,1.3832298401835303,7.0,1.0],[60.66276379306651,0.3930348838847877,7.0,0.0],[58.47775688905939,2.1382811420532613,7.0,1.0],[59.45066045416481,2.017603432410032,7.0,2.0],[58.484005878915795,3.5674345203377857,7.0,2.0],[56.48936047927191,0.4737055578728213,7.0,2.0],[54.06970348957429,1.551617977511754,7.0,1.0],[38.591456165013014,0.3422431327205233,7.0,2.0],[58.31743